'''Hauraki-Waikato''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[Māori electorates|Māori electorate]] first established for the {{NZ election link|2008}}. It largely replaced the {{NZ electorate link|Tainui}} electorate. [[Nanaia Mahuta]] of the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]], formerly the MP for Tainui, became MP for Hauraki-Waikato in the 2008 general election and was re-elected in {{NZ election link year|2011}}, {{NZ election link year|2014}}, {{NZ election link year|2017}} and {{NZ election link year|2020}} before being [[unseating|unseated]] by [[Hana-Rawhiti Maipi-Clarke]] in {{NZ election link year|2023}}.

==Population centres==
The electorate includes the following population centres:
[[Image:Hlz victoriast.jpg|thumb|right|Downtown [[Hamilton, New Zealand|Hamilton]]]]
Within the [[Auckland Region]]: [[Papakura]], [[Pukekohe]], [[Waiuku]], [[Clarks Beach]], [[Ramarama, New Zealand|Ramarama]], [[Bombay Hills|Bombay]], [[Pōkeno]].

Within the [[Waikato]] region: [[Meremere]], [[Huntly, New Zealand|Huntly]], [[Whitianga]], [[Whangamatā]], [[Thames, New Zealand|Thames]], [[Paeroa]], [[Waihi]], [[Hamilton, New Zealand|Hamilton]], [[Ngāruawāhia]], [[Morrinsville]], [[Matamata]], [[Cambridge, New Zealand|Cambridge]], [[Te Awamutu]], [[Raglan, New Zealand|Raglan]], [[Kawhia]].

In the 2007 boundary redistribution, the Tainui electorate was reduced in size by transferring the tribal area of [[Ngāti Maniapoto]] to the [[Te Tai Hauāuru]] electorate, and in the process, the electorate was renamed as Hauraki-Waikato.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2007 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10061-8 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=1 October 2014 |page=11|date=14 September 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190123193826/https://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |archive-date=23 January 2019 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The electorate saw no boundary adjustment in the 2013/14 redistribution.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=1 October 2014 |page=11|date=4 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006075102/http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |archive-date=6 October 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

In 2020, following the relatively higher population growth in the Hauraki-Waikato electorate than that of [[Tāmaki Makaurau (New Zealand electorate)|Tāmaki Makaurau]], Hauraki-Waikato's northern boundary was contracted to east of [[Manurewa]].<ref name="Report 2020">{{Cite book |last=Representation Commission |url=https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/historical-events/boundary-review-2019-2020/read-the-report-of-the-representation-commission-2020/ |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2020 |year=2020 |isbn=978-0-473-51728-1 |location=Wellington |pages=21 |oclc=1162791915 |access-date=28 June 2022}}</ref> Following an objection raised by the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] which emphasised [[Waiheke Island]]'s ferry connections to Auckland, the island was moved to Tāmaki Makaurau.<ref name="Report 2020" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=New Zealand Labour Party |date=December 2019 |title=New Zealand Labour Party Submission to the Representation Commission |url=https://elections.nz/assets/objection-form-uploads/Labour-Party-Submission-to-Representation-Commission-Dec-2019-M02-001.pdf |website=Elections NZ}}</ref>

==Tribal areas==
The electorate includes the following tribal areas: [[Ngāi Tai]], [[Ngāti Huia]], [[Ngāti Mahuta]], [[Ngāti Maru (Hauraki)|Ngāti Maru]], [[Ngāti Paoa]], [[Ngāti Raukawa]], [[Ngāti Tamaterā]], [[Ngāti Te Ata]], [[Tainui|Waikato-Tainui]], [[Ngāti Porou ki Hauraki]], [[Ngāti Hako]] and [[Ngāti Tara Tokanui]]

==History==
The electorate was originally proposed by [[Elections New Zealand]] as "Pare Hauraki-Pare Waikato"{{Efn|Translation: Tainui tribes of Hauraki – Tainui tribes of Waikato<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.maoridictionary.co.nz/index.cfm?dictionaryKeywords=Pare&search.x=33&search.y=2&n=1&idiom=0&phrase=0&proverb=0&loan=0 |title=Māori Dictionary – "Pare" |access-date=13 January 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130523161844/http://www.maoridictionary.co.nz/index.cfm?dictionaryKeywords=Pare&search.x=33&search.y=2&n=1&idiom=0&phrase=0&proverb=0&loan=0 |archive-date=23 May 2013 |url-status=dead }}</ref>}} to even out the numbers on the voting roll in [[Tainui (New Zealand electorate)|Tainui]] and [[Te Tai Hauauru]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/mapping/report/pare_hauraki_pare_waikato.html |title=Proposed Electorate Boundaries – Pare Hauraki-Pare Waikato |work=Elections New Zealand |access-date=10 April 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100514131834/http://www.elections.org.nz/mapping/report/pare_hauraki_pare_waikato.html |archive-date=14 May 2010}}</ref> Labour's [[Nanaia Mahuta]] won the {{NZ election link|2008}} against [[Angeline Greensill]] of the [[Māori Party]].<ref name="2008 election" /> In the {{NZ election link|2011}}, Mahuta defeated Greensill with a greatly increased margin of 35.5% of the candidate vote.<ref name="2011 election" /> Mahuta won the {{NZ election link|2014}} with another decisive majority.<ref>{{cite news |title=Nanaia Mahuta and Kelvin Davis consider what lies ahead for Māori Labour MPs |url= https://www.maoritelevision.com/news/politics/nanaia-mahuta-and-kelvin-davis-consider-what-lies-ahead-maori-labour-mps |access-date=2 October 2014 |work=[[Māori Television]] |date=21 September 2014}}</ref>

